 "name": "Urban Park Blue Space: Preference and Restoration",
 "description": "Faithful replication of Luo, S., Xie, J., & Furuya, K. (2021). Assessing the preference and restorative potential of urban park blue space. Land 10(11):1233. https://doi.org/10.3390/land10111233\n\nParticipants see the paper's ten photographs of blue spaces (creeks, channels and ponds) in Huanhuaxi Park, Chengdu, one at a time in random order. For each, they imagine being in the scene and rate seven statements from 1 (totally disagree) to 7 (totally agree): the five-item short Perceived Restorativeness Scale (fascination, compatibility, being away, scope, coherence), \"The landscape is beautiful\" (aesthetic preference) and \"This place is natural\" (perceived naturalness), as in the paper's Table 2. They then give age, gender, education, major and childhood living environment.\n\nOriginal study: 93 university students in a lab, one minute per slide (October 2020). Image 5, a large pond surrounded by bamboo and traditional buildings, was most preferred (5.67) and most restorative (5.10); image 2, a small channel beside a nature trail, was least preferred (4.25) and least restorative (4.20); image 7 was rated most natural. Demographic groups did not differ. Clean, natural-looking water and diverse vegetation predicted preference and restorative potential.\n\nAnalysis as in the paper: restorative potential = mean of the five PRS items; means per image; Mann–Whitney / Kruskal–Wallis tests across demographic groups; correlations and regressions with landscape features (the experts' feature scores of the paper's Table 3 can be added as image attributes).\n\nDifferences from the original study: (1) online and self-paced instead of a group lab session with one minute per projected slide (the instructions suggest about a minute per picture); (2) the images are the versions printed in the paper's Table 1, smaller than the projected slides; (3) English wording of the items follows the paper's Table 2 (the original questionnaire was in Chinese); (4) the expert rating of landscape characteristics (Table 3) is not part of this template.\n\nImages: Table 1 of the open-access article (CC BY 4.0), shown with credit for research; the template is removed if the authors object.",
